Abstract = {The aim of this study was to determine if dimensions and sub-categories
   of a previously-tested obtrusiveness framework were represented in
   interviews conducted with community-dwelling older adults at three-and
   six-month study visits during an in-home sensor study. Secondary
   analysis of interviews was performed using a codebook based on an
   obtrusiveness framework. Eight community-dwelling older adults aged
   79-86 participated in 15 interviews. One participant died between the
   three-and six-month interviews. Some elements of the obtrusiveness
   framework were present at three months but not at six months, indicating
   that perceptions of obtrusiveness of in-home sensors may decline over
   time. Findings highlight the importance of privacy issues and perceived
   usefulness for sensor technology use and adoption. There is a need to
   develop an obtrusiveness assessment instrument that enables nuanced
   measurements based on specific contexts and types of technologies.},
